{"toolCallId":"call_345e400fc64046d1bed2528e96813d83","title":"Ran node","kind":"execute","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"resource","resource":{"mimeType":"text/x-shellscript","text":"node -e \"\nconst fs=require('fs');\nconst j=JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/validator-registry.json','utf8'));\nfor (const r of j) console.log([r.rule_id, r.severity, r.strict_only?'STRICT':'', r.script_path].join(' | '));\n\"","uri":"tool://preview"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/preview_is_shell_command":true}}}],"rawInput":{"command":"node -e \"\nconst fs=require('fs');\nconst j=JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/validator-registry.json','utf8'));\nfor (const r of j) console.log([r.rule_id, r.severity, r.strict_only?'STRICT':'', r.script_path].join(' | '));\n\"","workdir":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8f3d32b7e3/tree"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/commandNames":["node"],"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"exec"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_37b8014595cf46748b36d71280f39531","title":"Read file","kind":"read","locations":[{"path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8f3d32b7e3/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/plan.md"}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8f3d32b7e3/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/plan.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"read"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_3846789314914a56b9967f8cd3436587","title":"Edit file","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8f3d32b7e3/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/plan.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"Adds one global, one parser case, one shared level-line helper and one quiet renderer to recommend-level.sh. The default report is proven unchanged by a byte comparison against three captured baselines.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.3 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | Bash 3.2 or newer, `set -euo pipefail` |\n| **Framework** | None; one CLI script with a hand-rolled argument parser |\n| **Storage** | None |\n| **Testing** | `bash -n`, direct invocations, `wc -l` and `diff` against captured baselines |\n\n### Overview\n\nThe change touches one file and adds four small pieces to it: a `QUIET` global, a `--quiet` case in\nthe argument parser, a `format_level_line()` helper shared by both text renderers, and an\n`output_quiet()` function dispatched from section 7. Scoring, phase logic and the JSON renderer are\nuntouched, so the default report can only change if the helper's format string differs from the\n`echo` it replaces, which a byte comparison against three pre-change captures catches.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n\n### Definition of Ready\n- [x] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [x] Success criteria measurable\n- [x] Dependencies identified\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] Tests passing (if applicable)\n- [ ] Docs updated (spec/plan/tasks)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n\n### Pattern\nSingle-script CLI: parse arguments, score, then dispatch to one renderer.\n\n### Key Components\n- **Argument parser (section 5)**: recognizes `--quiet`, sets `QUIET=true`, and keeps its existing `shift` discipline so no later case sees the flag.\n- **`format_level_line()` (section 4)**: the one place that spells `Recommended Level: N (Name)`, called by both text renderers.\n- **`output_quiet()` (section 4)**: prints that one line and nothing else.\n- **Output dispatch (section 7)**: `--json` first, then `QUIET`, then the default `output_text`.\n\n### Data Flow\n\n`argv` enters the parser, which sets `LOC`, `FILES`, the four risk flags, `JSON_OUTPUT` and `QUIET`.\nSection 6 computes `SCORE_LOC`, `SCORE_FILES`, `SCORE_RISK`, `SCORE_COMPLEXITY` and `TOTAL_SCORE`,\nthen `determine_level` sets `RECOMMENDED_LEVEL` and `LEVEL_NAME`. Section 7 picks one renderer, which\nwrites to stdout. The flag is read only at dispatch, so it cannot influence a score.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:architecture -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:affected-surfaces -->\n## FIX ADDENDUM: AFFECTED SURFACES\n\nThis is a feature addition, not a bug fix, but the output surface is shared, so the inventory is\nrecorded here rather than skipped.\n\n| Surface | Current Role | Action | Verification |\n|---------|--------------|--------|--------------|\n| `recommend-level.sh` `output_text` | owns the 12-17 line report | update: call `format_level_line` instead of its inline `echo` | `diff` against the three captured baselines exits 0 |\n| `recommend-level.sh` `output_json` | owns the 36-line JSON document | unchanged | `--json` output still parses and matches `recommended_level` |\n| `recommend-level.sh` argument parser | owns option recognition | update: one `--quiet` case | `rg -n -- '--quiet\\)' recommend-level.sh` prints one line |\n| `recommend-level.sh` header comment and `--help` | document the option set | update: list `--quiet` in both | `rg -n -- '--quiet' recommend-level.sh` names both sites |\n\nRequired inventories:\n- Same-class producers: `rg -n 'Recommended Level:' .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` must list every site that renders the label; the extraction is complete only when that list has no inline copy left outside the helper.\n- Consumers of changed symbols: `rg -n 'recommend-level' .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit --glob '*.sh' --glob '*.md'` names each caller the flag could affect.\n- Matrix axes: three input shapes (`--loc 50 --files 2`, `--loc 500 --files 15`, `--loc 500 --files 15 --auth --api --db --architectural`) crossed with three output modes (default, `--quiet`, `--json`), nine rows, of which the six text rows are asserted.\n- Algorithm invariant: the flag is read only at dispatch, so `TOTAL_SCORE`, `RECOMMENDED_LEVEL`, `LEVEL_NAME`, `CONFIDENCE` and every phase field are computed identically with and without it.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:affected-surfaces -->\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phases -->\n## 4. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\nFollow the ordered tasks in `tasks.md`. It owns the Setup, Implementation and Verification phase checkboxes and task state. Setup captures the baselines, Implementation adds the helper and the renderer, Verification diffs both output shapes and runs the packet validator.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phases -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## 5. TESTING STRATEGY\n\n| Test Type | Scope | Tools |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| Unit | `format_level_line` and `output_quiet` through direct invocations | `bash`, `wc -l` |\n| Integration | Parser, scoring and dispatch across three input shapes | `diff` against `scratch/baseline/`, `bash -n` |\n| Manual | `--help` text, the unknown-option path and `--quiet --json` | Terminal run |\n\nThe three assertions that decide the packet:\n\n1. `bash recommend-level.sh --loc 500 --files 15 --quiet | wc -l` prints `1`.\n2. `bash recommend-level.sh --loc 500 --files 15 | wc -l` prints `16`, and the text matches the baseline byte for byte.\n3. `bash recommend-level.sh --loc 500 --files 15 --json --quiet | wc -l` prints `36`, the same as `--json` alone.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n## 6. DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Dependency | Type | Status | Impact if Blocked |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------------|\n| `bash` 3.2 or newer | Internal | Green | The script already requires it; no change |\n| `diff` and `wc` | Internal | Green | Baseline comparison unavailable; fall back to reading both outputs line by line |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:rollback -->\n## 7. ROLLBACK PLAN\n\n- **Trigger**: The default report differs from a baseline capture, or `--quiet` changes a computed level or score.\n- **Procedure**: Revert the single commit that touched `recommend-level.sh` and rerun the three baseline invocations. The change is confined to one file with no data, config or schema to unwind.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n## L2: PHASE DEPENDENCIES\n\n```\nPhase 1 (Setup) ──────► Phase 2 (Implementation) ──────► Phase 3 (Verification)\n```\n\n| Phase | Depends On | Blocks |\n|-------|------------|--------|\n| Setup | None | Implementation, Verification |\n| Implementation | Setup | Verification |\n| Verification | Implementation | None |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:effort -->\n## L2: EFFORT ESTIMATION\n\n| Phase | Complexity | Estimated Effort |\n|-------|------------|------------------|\n| Setup | Low | 10 minutes |\n| Core Implementation | Low | 30 minutes |\n| Verification | Low | 15 minutes |\n| **Total** | | **About 1 hour (assumption, not a commitment)** |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:effort -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n## L2: ENHANCED ROLLBACK\n\n### Pre-deployment Checklist\n- [ ] Backup created (if data changes) - no data changes\n- [ ] Feature flag configured - the flag itself is the feature; no rollout flag\n- [ ] Monitoring alerts set - no service to monitor\n\n### Rollback Procedure\n1. Revert the commit that touched `recommend-level.sh`.\n2. Rerun the three baseline invocations and confirm the report matches the captures.\n3. Confirm `--quiet` is gone: the parser exits 1 with `ERROR: Unknown option: --quiet`.\n\n### Data Reversal\n- **Has data migrations?** No\n- **Reversal procedure**: N/A - the script persists nothing\n<!-- /ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n## L3: DEPENDENCY GRAPH\n\n```\n┌─────────────┐     ┌─────────────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐\n│   Phase 1   │────►│      Phase 2        │────►│   Phase 3   │\n│   Setup     │     │ format_level_line   │     │   Verify    │\n│  baselines  │     │ output_quiet + case │     │  diff + wc  │\n└─────────────┘     └─────────────────────┘     └─────────────┘\n```\n\n### Dependency Matrix\n\n| Component | Depends On | Produces | Blocks |\n|-----------|------------|----------|--------|\n| Baseline captures | None | Three reference outputs | Default-report diff |\n| `format_level_line` | Baseline captures | One shared label | `output_text` edit, `output_quiet` |\n| `output_quiet` and parser case | `format_level_line` | The quiet path | Verification |\n| Verification | All of the above | Line counts and a clean diff | Packet closure |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n## L3: CRITICAL PATH\n\n1. **Capture three baselines** - 10 minutes - CRITICAL\n2. **Extract the helper and add the renderer** - 30 minutes - CRITICAL\n3. **Diff both output shapes and run the validator** - 15 minutes - CRITICAL\n\n**Total Critical Path**: About 55 minutes.\n\n**Parallel Opportunities**:\n- The header comment and `--help` edit can proceed alongside the renderer work, because neither affects stdout.\n- The consumer inventory (`rg -n 'recommend-level'`) can run during Setup, since it reads only.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:milestones -->\n## L3: MILESTONES\n\n| Milestone | Description | Success Criteria | Target |\n|-----------|-------------|------------------|--------|\n| M1 | Baselines captured | Three files under `scratch/baseline/` whose `wc -l` reads 16, 17 and 12 | Phase 1 |\n| M2 | Quiet path works | `--quiet --loc 500 --files 15 \\| wc -l` prints `1` | Phase 2 |\n| M3 | Default report proven unchanged | `diff` against each baseline exits 0 and `validate.sh --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED` | Phase 3 |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:milestones -->\n\n---\n\n## L3: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD\n\n### ADR-001: A separate `output_quiet` renderer, not a branch inside `output_text`\n\n**Status**: Accepted\n\n**Context**: `output_text` builds the whole report, and a quiet branch inside it would sit in the middle of a function whose single job is the full report. The default report is the part of this change most likely to regress, so the safest structure keeps it untouched.\n\n**Decision**: Add `output_quiet()` beside `output_text()` and `output_json()`, and let section 7 choose between them. Extract the label into `format_level_line()` so both text renderers print the same string from one format.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- The default report's body is unchanged; only its level line becomes a call to the shared helper, and the byte comparison proves the string is identical.\n- Three renderers now exist, so a future label change must edit the helper rather than one `echo`, which is the point of the extraction.\n\n**Alternatives Rejected**:\n- A `if [[ \"$QUIET\" = true ]]` branch at the top of `output_text`: rejected because every future edit to the report would have to reason about two modes in one function.\n- An environment variable instead of a flag: rejected because every existing caller already passes flags, and an env var would add a second configuration surface to test.\n\n---\n\n### ADR-002: `--json` wins over `--quiet`\n\n**Status**: Accepted\n\n**Context**: Both flags ask for a different output shape. A silent winner is unavoidable when both are passed, so the choice has to be deliberate and documented.\n\n**Decision**: Section 7 dispatches `JSON_OUTPUT` first, then `QUIET`, then the default text renderer. `--quiet` therefore applies only to the text report.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- A pipeline that adds `--quiet` to an existing `--json` call keeps working and keeps parsing JSON.\n- `--quiet --json` is a documented no-op for `--quiet`, which callers must read once.\n\n**Alternatives Rejected**:\n- Erroring when both are passed: rejected because it turns a harmless combination into a failure for scripts that set flags generically.\n- Letting `--quiet` win: rejected because it would silently drop a machine-readable contract that already exists.\n\n---\n\n## AI EXECUTION PROTOCOL\n\n### Pre-Task Checklist\n\nBefore starting a task, the conductor confirms four things and records each as a line in its\nprogress note:\n\n- The task id and its `Verify:` command are read from `tasks.md`, not from memory.\n- The baseline captures from T002 exist, because every later claim about the default report\n  depends on them.\n- Only `recommend-level.sh` is in scope; the packet documents are not edited during\n  implementation.\n- `bash -n` passes on the current file, so a later failure can be attributed to the change.\n\n### Execution Rules\n\n| Rule | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| TASK-SEQ | Tasks run in id order. T006 must precede T007, because the renderer calls the helper. |\n| TASK-SCOPE | One file changes: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh`. A task that needs a second file stops and reports. |\n| TASK-EVIDENCE | A task is complete only when its `Verify:` command was run and its output read, not when the edit was applied. |\n\n### Status Reporting Format\n\nEach task reports one line, in this shape:\n\n`T006 done | verify: diff baseline exits 0 | observed: no output | next: T007`\n\nThe status word is one of `done`, `blocked` or `skipped`, and the observed field quotes the\ncommand's actual output rather than a summary of it.\n\n### Blocked Task Protocol\n\nA task is marked `[B]` and reported as `blocked` when its `Verify:` command fails after two\nattempts, when the edit anchor no longer matches the file, or when completing it would require a\nfile outside TASK-SCOPE. A blocked task stops the sequence: later tasks that depend on it are not\nstarted, and the report names the failing command and its output. The conductor does not widen\nscope to clear a block on its own.\n\n---\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"Adds one global, one parser case, one shared level-line helper and one quiet renderer to recommend-level.sh. The default report is proven unchanged by a byte comparison against three captured baselines.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.3 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | Bash 3.2 or newer, `set -euo pipefail` |\n| **Framework** | None; one CLI script with a hand-rolled argument parser |\n| **Storage** | None |\n| **Testing** | `bash -n`, direct invocations, `wc -l` and `diff` against captured baselines |\n\n### Overview\n\nThe change touches one file and adds four small pieces to it: a `QUIET` global, a `--quiet` case in\nthe argument parser, a `format_level_line()` helper shared by both text renderers, and an\n`output_quiet()` function dispatched from section 7. Scoring, phase logic and the JSON renderer are\nuntouched, so the default report can only change if the helper's format string differs from the\n`echo` it replaces, which a byte comparison against three pre-change captures catches.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n\n### Definition of Ready\n- [x] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [x] Success criteria measurable\n- [x] Dependencies identified\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] Tests passing (if applicable)\n- [ ] Docs updated (spec/plan/tasks)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n\n### Pattern\nSingle-script CLI: parse arguments, score, then dispatch to one renderer.\n\n### Key Components\n- **Argument parser (section 5)**: recognizes `--quiet`, sets `QUIET=true`, and keeps its existing `shift` discipline so no later case sees the flag.\n- **`format_level_line()` (section 4)**: the one place that spells `Recommended Level: N (Name)`, called by both text renderers.\n- **`output_quiet()` (section 4)**: prints that one line and nothing else.\n- **Output dispatch (section 7)**: `--json` first, then `QUIET`, then the default `output_text`.\n\n### Data Flow\n\n`argv` enters the parser, which sets `LOC`, `FILES`, the four risk flags, `JSON_OUTPUT` and `QUIET`.\nSection 6 computes `SCORE_LOC`, `SCORE_FILES`, `SCORE_RISK`, `SCORE_COMPLEXITY` and `TOTAL_SCORE`,\nthen `determine_level` sets `RECOMMENDED_LEVEL` and `LEVEL_NAME`. Section 7 picks one renderer, which\nwrites to stdout. The flag is read only at dispatch, so it cannot influence a score.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:architecture -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:affected-surfaces -->\n## FIX ADDENDUM: AFFECTED SURFACES\n\nThis is a feature addition, not a bug fix, but the output surface is shared, so the inventory is\nrecorded here rather than skipped.\n\n| Surface | Current Role | Action | Verification |\n|---------|--------------|--------|--------------|\n| `recommend-level.sh` `output_text` | owns the 12-18 line report | update: call `format_level_line` instead of its inline `echo` | `diff` against the three captured baselines exits 0 |\n| `recommend-level.sh` `output_json` | owns the 36-line JSON document | unchanged | `--json` output still parses and matches `recommended_level` |\n| `recommend-level.sh` argument parser | owns option recognition | update: one `--quiet` case | `rg -n -- '--quiet\\)' recommend-level.sh` prints one line |\n| `recommend-level.sh` header comment and `--help` | document the option set | update: list `--quiet` in both | `rg -n -- '--quiet' recommend-level.sh` names both sites |\n\nRequired inventories:\n- Same-class producers: `rg -n 'Recommended Level:' .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` must list every site that renders the label; the extraction is complete only when that list has no inline copy left outside the helper.\n- Consumers of changed symbols: `rg -n 'recommend-level' .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit --glob '*.sh' --glob '*.md'` names each caller the flag could affect.\n- Matrix axes: three input shapes (`--loc 50 --files 2`, `--loc 500 --files 15`, `--loc 500 --files 15 --auth --api --db --architectural`) crossed with three output modes (default, `--quiet`, `--json`), nine rows, of which the six text rows are asserted.\n- Algorithm invariant: the flag is read only at dispatch, so `TOTAL_SCORE`, `RECOMMENDED_LEVEL`, `LEVEL_NAME`, `CONFIDENCE` and every phase field are computed identically with and without it.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:affected-surfaces -->\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phases -->\n## 4. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\nFollow the ordered tasks in `tasks.md`. It owns the Setup, Implementation and Verification phase checkboxes and task state. Setup captures the baselines, Implementation adds the helper and the renderer, Verification diffs both output shapes and runs the packet validator.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phases -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## 5. TESTING STRATEGY\n\n| Test Type | Scope | Tools |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| Unit | `format_level_line` and `output_quiet` through direct invocations | `bash`, `wc -l` |\n| Integration | Parser, scoring and dispatch across three input shapes | `diff` against `scratch/baseline/`, `bash -n` |\n| Manual | `--help` text, the unknown-option path and `--quiet --json` | Terminal run |\n\nThe three assertions that decide the packet:\n\n1. `bash recommend-level.sh --loc 500 --files 15 --quiet | wc -l` prints `1`.\n2. `bash recommend-level.sh --loc 500 --files 15 | wc -l` prints `16`, and the text matches the baseline byte for byte.\n3. `bash recommend-level.sh --loc 500 --files 15 --json --quiet | wc -l` prints `36`, the same as `--json` alone.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n## 6. DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Dependency | Type | Status | Impact if Blocked |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------------|\n| `bash` 3.2 or newer | Internal | Green | The script already requires it; no change |\n| `diff` and `wc` | Internal | Green | Baseline comparison unavailable; fall back to reading both outputs line by line |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:rollback -->\n## 7. ROLLBACK PLAN\n\n- **Trigger**: The default report differs from a baseline capture, or `--quiet` changes a computed level or score.\n- **Procedure**: Revert the single commit that touched `recommend-level.sh` and rerun the three baseline invocations. The change is confined to one file with no data, config or schema to unwind.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n## L2: PHASE DEPENDENCIES\n\n```\nPhase 1 (Setup) ──────► Phase 2 (Implementation) ──────► Phase 3 (Verification)\n```\n\n| Phase | Depends On | Blocks |\n|-------|------------|--------|\n| Setup | None | Implementation, Verification |\n| Implementation | Setup | Verification |\n| Verification | Implementation | None |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:effort -->\n## L2: EFFORT ESTIMATION\n\n| Phase | Complexity | Estimated Effort |\n|-------|------------|------------------|\n| Setup | Low | 10 minutes |\n| Core Implementation | Low | 30 minutes |\n| Verification | Low | 15 minutes |\n| **Total** | | **About 1 hour (assumption, not a commitment)** |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:effort -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n## L2: ENHANCED ROLLBACK\n\n### Pre-deployment Checklist\n- [ ] Backup created (if data changes) - no data changes\n- [ ] Feature flag configured - the flag itself is the feature; no rollout flag\n- [ ] Monitoring alerts set - no service to monitor\n\n### Rollback Procedure\n1. Revert the commit that touched `recommend-level.sh`.\n2. Rerun the three baseline invocations and confirm the report matches the captures.\n3. Confirm `--quiet` is gone: the parser exits 1 with `ERROR: Unknown option: --quiet`.\n\n### Data Reversal\n- **Has data migrations?** No\n- **Reversal procedure**: N/A - the script persists nothing\n<!-- /ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n## L3: DEPENDENCY GRAPH\n\n```\n┌─────────────┐     ┌─────────────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐\n│   Phase 1   │────►│      Phase 2        │────►│   Phase 3   │\n│   Setup     │     │ format_level_line   │     │   Verify    │\n│  baselines  │     │ output_quiet + case │     │  diff + wc  │\n└─────────────┘     └─────────────────────┘     └─────────────┘\n```\n\n### Dependency Matrix\n\n| Component | Depends On | Produces | Blocks |\n|-----------|------------|----------|--------|\n| Baseline captures | None | Three reference outputs | Default-report diff |\n| `format_level_line` | Baseline captures | One shared label | `output_text` edit, `output_quiet` |\n| `output_quiet` and parser case | `format_level_line` | The quiet path | Verification |\n| Verification | All of the above | Line counts and a clean diff | Packet closure |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n## L3: CRITICAL PATH\n\n1. **Capture three baselines** - 10 minutes - CRITICAL\n2. **Extract the helper and add the renderer** - 30 minutes - CRITICAL\n3. **Diff both output shapes and run the validator** - 15 minutes - CRITICAL\n\n**Total Critical Path**: About 55 minutes.\n\n**Parallel Opportunities**:\n- The header comment and `--help` edit can proceed alongside the renderer work, because neither affects stdout.\n- The consumer inventory (`rg -n 'recommend-level'`) can run during Setup, since it reads only.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:milestones -->\n## L3: MILESTONES\n\n| Milestone | Description | Success Criteria | Target |\n|-----------|-------------|------------------|--------|\n| M1 | Baselines captured | Three files under `scratch/baseline/` whose `wc -l` reads 16, 17 and 12 | Phase 1 |\n| M2 | Quiet path works | `--quiet --loc 500 --files 15 \\| wc -l` prints `1` | Phase 2 |\n| M3 | Default report proven unchanged | `diff` against each baseline exits 0 and `validate.sh --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED` | Phase 3 |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:milestones -->\n\n---\n\n## L3: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD\n\n### ADR-001: A separate `output_quiet` renderer, not a branch inside `output_text`\n\n**Status**: Accepted\n\n**Context**: `output_text` builds the whole report, and a quiet branch inside it would sit in the middle of a function whose single job is the full report. The default report is the part of this change most likely to regress, so the safest structure keeps it untouched.\n\n**Decision**: Add `output_quiet()` beside `output_text()` and `output_json()`, and let section 7 choose between them. Extract the label into `format_level_line()` so both text renderers print the same string from one format.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- The default report's body is unchanged; only its level line becomes a call to the shared helper, and the byte comparison proves the string is identical.\n- Three renderers now exist, so a future label change must edit the helper rather than one `echo`, which is the point of the extraction.\n\n**Alternatives Rejected**:\n- A `if [[ \"$QUIET\" = true ]]` branch at the top of `output_text`: rejected because every future edit to the report would have to reason about two modes in one function.\n- An environment variable instead of a flag: rejected because every existing caller already passes flags, and an env var would add a second configuration surface to test.\n\n---\n\n### ADR-002: `--json` wins over `--quiet`\n\n**Status**: Accepted\n\n**Context**: Both flags ask for a different output shape. A silent winner is unavoidable when both are passed, so the choice has to be deliberate and documented.\n\n**Decision**: Section 7 dispatches `JSON_OUTPUT` first, then `QUIET`, then the default text renderer. `--quiet` therefore applies only to the text report.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- A pipeline that adds `--quiet` to an existing `--json` call keeps working and keeps parsing JSON.\n- `--quiet --json` is a documented no-op for `--quiet`, which callers must read once.\n\n**Alternatives Rejected**:\n- Erroring when both are passed: rejected because it turns a harmless combination into a failure for scripts that set flags generically.\n- Letting `--quiet` win: rejected because it would silently drop a machine-readable contract that already exists.\n\n---\n\n## AI EXECUTION PROTOCOL\n\n### Pre-Task Checklist\n\nBefore starting a task, the conductor confirms four things and records each as a line in its\nprogress note:\n\n- The task id and its `Verify:` command are read from `tasks.md`, not from memory.\n- The baseline captures from T002 exist, because every later claim about the default report\n  depends on them.\n- Only `recommend-level.sh` is in scope; the packet documents are not edited during\n  implementation.\n- `bash -n` passes on the current file, so a later failure can be attributed to the change.\n\n### Execution Rules\n\n| Rule | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| TASK-SEQ | Tasks run in id order. T006 must precede T007, because the renderer calls the helper. |\n| TASK-SCOPE | One file changes: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh`. A task that needs a second file stops and reports. |\n| TASK-EVIDENCE | A task is complete only when its `Verify:` command was run and its output read, not when the edit was applied. |\n\n### Status Reporting Format\n\nEach task reports one line, in this shape:\n\n`T006 done | verify: diff baseline exits 0 | observed: no output | next: T007`\n\nThe status word is one of `done`, `blocked` or `skipped`, and the observed field quotes the\ncommand's actual output rather than a summary of it.\n\n### Blocked Task Protocol\n\nA task is marked `[B]` and reported as `blocked` when its `Verify:` command fails after two\nattempts, when the edit anchor no longer matches the file, or when completing it would require a\nfile outside TASK-SCOPE. A blocked task stops the sequence: later tasks that depend on it are not\nstarted, and the report names the failing command and its output. The conductor does not widen\nscope to clear a block on its own.\n\n---\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":1,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":1}}],"locations":[{"path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8f3d32b7e3/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/plan.md"}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8f3d32b7e3/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/plan.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"edit"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_3873ff02ca1347b4af71a86a042ea6f9","title":"Read lines 1-120 in ./.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/validation/spec-doc-structure.ts","kind":"execute","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"resource","resource":{"mimeType":"text/x-shellscript","text":"sed -n '1,120p' .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/validation/spec-doc-structure.ts","uri":"tool://preview"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/preview_is_shell_command":true}}}],"rawInput":{"command":"sed -n '1,120p' .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/validation/spec-doc-structure.ts","workdir":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8f3d32b7e3/tree"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/commandNames":["sed"],"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"exec"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_3c093a7d02d543cdae174f29dfac2e92","title":"Read file","kind":"read","locations":[{"path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8f3d32b7e3/tree/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/validation/validation-rules.md"}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8f3d32b7e3/tree/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/validation/validation-rules.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"read"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_3d8c3782252e4974a8862f4fee18131a","title":"Edit file","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8f3d32b7e3/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"recommend-level.sh answers one question but prints a 16-line report to do it. A --quiet flag prints the recommended-level line alone, and the default report stays byte-identical.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n`recommend-level.sh` exists to answer one question, yet every invocation prints a 16-line text\nreport: a level line, a score line, a four-line breakdown and a phase-recommendation block. A\ncaller that needs only the level parses the second line of that report or asks for `--json` and\npulls `recommended_level` out of a 36-line document. Adding `--quiet` prints the single\n`Recommended Level:` line and leaves the default output byte-identical, which a line count on both\ninvocations proves.\n\n**Key Decisions**: A separate `output_quiet` renderer, so the default report is never edited in\nplace; `--json` wins when both output flags are passed.\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: None beyond `bash` 3.2 or newer, which the script already targets.\n\n---\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P1 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n\nEvery invocation of `recommend-level.sh` prints a 16-line text report: a level line, a score line,\na four-line factor breakdown and a phase-recommendation block. A caller that only needs the level,\nsuch as a scaffolder choosing `--level` or a shell test asserting a recommendation, must either\nparse the second line of that report or request `--json` and pull `recommended_level` out of a\n36-line JSON document. Both routes make the caller depend on a shape it does not want.\n\n### Purpose\n\nGive callers a one-line answer that is the same string the full report already prints, without\nchanging a byte of what the full report prints.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n\n- Add a `--quiet` option that prints only the recommended-level line and exits 0.\n- Keep the level label in one place, so the quiet line and the default report cannot drift apart.\n- Dispatch the output mode in section 7 of the script, with `--json` taking precedence.\n- Document the flag in the header comment block and in the `--help` text.\n- Verify both output shapes with line counts and a byte comparison of the default report.\n\n### Out of Scope\n\n- Changing scoring, thresholds or phase logic - the flag must not alter the recommendation.\n- Adding a short `-q` alias - the script's short aliases are `-j` and `-h`, and a new one is a\n  separate decision with its own collision risk.\n- Suppressing stderr - errors and warnings stay visible, because `--quiet` governs stdout only.\n- Changing the JSON schema - `--json` output is untouched by this packet.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | Add the flag, the quiet renderer, the shared level-line helper and the help text |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\nREQ ids run in one sequence across the P0 and P1 tables. A new requirement takes the highest REQ id plus one. validate.sh does not link ids between files.\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | `--quiet` writes exactly one line to stdout, `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)`, and exits 0. |\n| REQ-002 | Without `--quiet`, stdout is byte-identical to the current output for the same arguments. |\n| REQ-003 | `--quiet` changes no computed value: level, score, confidence and phase fields are identical to the default run's. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-004 | `--quiet` together with `--json` emits the JSON document unchanged; `--json` wins and `--quiet` is a no-op for that invocation. |\n| REQ-005 | `--help` lists `--quiet` and still exits 0, and every error path still writes to stderr and exits 1. |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: `bash recommend-level.sh --loc 500 --files 15 --quiet | wc -l` prints `1`.\n- **SC-002**: `bash recommend-level.sh --loc 500 --files 15 | wc -l` prints `16`, and that output matches the pre-change capture byte for byte.\n- **SC-003**: For `--loc 50 --files 2`, `--loc 500 --files 15` and `--loc 500 --files 15 --auth --api --db --architectural`, the quiet line equals the level line the default report prints.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | `bash` 3.2 or newer | The script cannot run at all on an older shell; nothing new is introduced | The script already targets that floor and uses `set -euo pipefail` today |\n| Risk | The default report regresses while the shared level-line helper is extracted | Med | Capture three baseline invocations before editing and diff them after |\n| Risk | A caller assumes `--quiet` also silences stderr | Low | Document stdout-only scope and test an error path with `--quiet` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: `--quiet` adds no measurable time; the quiet path is one `printf` and skips the report, so it can only be faster than the default run.\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: N/A - the flag reads no input, opens no file and handles no secret; it adds no attack surface.\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: Output is deterministic for identical arguments; the change adds no state, no file I/O and no randomness.\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: `--quiet` with no `--loc` and no `--files` exits 1 with the existing usage error on stderr and writes nothing to stdout.\n- Maximum length: `--loc 100000 --files 5000` saturates every factor at its weight cap and still prints one line, with the same shape as any other quiet run.\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- External service failure: N/A - the script calls no external service.\n- Network timeout: N/A - the script performs no network access.\n\n### State Transitions\n- Partial completion: N/A - the script is a single pass with no persisted state.\n- Session expiry: N/A - the script has no session.\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 4/25 | Files: 1, LOC: ~25, Systems: 1 |\n| Risk | 5/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N (the default report is preserved) |\n| Research | 4/20 | Read one script and capture three baselines |\n| Multi-Agent | 0/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 2/15 | Dependencies: 0 |\n| **Total** | **15/100** | **Level 3 (assigned by dispatch)** |\n\nThe measured score is 15/100, well under the 70-point Level 3 threshold. The packet is authored at\nLevel 3 because the dispatch fixed the level before authoring began, and the score is recorded as\nmeasured rather than inflated to match the contract. The documentation contract, not the change's\nsize, is what the level describes here.\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | Extracting the shared level-line helper changes the default report | H | L | Add the helper first, diff the three baselines immediately, and keep the helper's format string identical to the current `echo` |\n| R-002 | A caller expects `--quiet` to hide stderr warnings | M | M | State stdout-only scope in `--help` and in this spec; test an error path under `--quiet` |\n| R-003 | `--quiet --json` silently picks a winner the caller did not expect | M | L | Define `--json` precedence explicitly, document it, and test the pair |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: One-line answer for scripts (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** scaffolder or shell test that needs only the level, **I want** `--quiet` to print the recommended-level line alone, **so that** I can read the answer with `$(...)` instead of parsing a report.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: Unchanged report for humans (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** developer running the script by hand, **I want** the default report to stay exactly as it is, **so that** a convenience flag cannot cost me the breakdown I read today.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-003: Predictable composition with the other flags (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** caller that combines output flags, **I want** `--json` and `--help` to keep their current behavior when `--quiet` is present, **so that** adding the flag never changes an existing pipeline's output.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- None blocking. Whether `--quiet` deserves a `-q` alias is deliberately out of scope here; a caller that needs one can ask for a follow-up packet.\n- None blocking. Whether stderr should ever be silenced is a separate decision; this packet leaves stderr untouched.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `decision-record.md`\n\n---\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"recommend-level.sh answers one question but prints a 16-line report to do it. A --quiet flag prints the recommended-level line alone, and the default report stays byte-identical.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n`recommend-level.sh` exists to answer one question, yet every invocation prints a 16-line text\nreport: a level line, a score line, a four-line breakdown and a phase-recommendation block. A\ncaller that needs only the level parses the second line of that report or asks for `--json` and\npulls `recommended_level` out of a 36-line document. Adding `--quiet` prints the single\n`Recommended Level:` line and leaves the default output byte-identical, which a line count on both\ninvocations proves.\n\n**Key Decisions**: A separate `output_quiet` renderer, so the default report is never edited in\nplace; `--json` wins when both output flags are passed.\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: None beyond `bash` 3.2 or newer, which the script already targets.\n\n---\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P1 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n\nEvery invocation of `recommend-level.sh` prints a 16-line text report: a level line, a score line,\na four-line factor breakdown and a phase-recommendation block. A caller that only needs the level,\nsuch as a scaffolder choosing `--level` or a shell test asserting a recommendation, must either\nparse the second line of that report or request `--json` and pull `recommended_level` out of a\n36-line JSON document. Both routes make the caller depend on a shape it does not want.\n\n### Purpose\n\nGive callers a one-line answer that is the same string the full report already prints, without\nchanging a byte of what the full report prints.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n\n- Add a `--quiet` option that prints only the recommended-level line and exits 0.\n- Keep the level label in one place, so the quiet line and the default report cannot drift apart.\n- Dispatch the output mode in section 7 of the script, with `--json` taking precedence.\n- Document the flag in the header comment block and in the `--help` text.\n- Verify both output shapes with line counts and a byte comparison of the default report.\n\n### Out of Scope\n\n- Changing scoring, thresholds or phase logic - the flag must not alter the recommendation.\n- Adding a short `-q` alias - the script's short aliases are `-j` and `-h`, and a new one is a\n  separate decision with its own collision risk.\n- Suppressing stderr - errors and warnings stay visible, because `--quiet` governs stdout only.\n- Changing the JSON schema - `--json` output is untouched by this packet.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | Add the flag, the quiet renderer, the shared level-line helper and the help text |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\nREQ ids run in one sequence across the P0 and P1 tables. A new requirement takes the highest REQ id plus one. validate.sh does not link ids between files.\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | `--quiet` writes exactly one line to stdout, in the form `Recommended Level: 2 (Verification)`, and exits 0. |\n| REQ-002 | Without `--quiet`, stdout is byte-identical to the current output for the same arguments. |\n| REQ-003 | `--quiet` changes no computed value: level, score, confidence and phase fields are identical to the default run's. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-004 | `--quiet` together with `--json` emits the JSON document unchanged; `--json` wins and `--quiet` is a no-op for that invocation. |\n| REQ-005 | `--help` lists `--quiet` and still exits 0, and every error path still writes to stderr and exits 1. |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: `bash recommend-level.sh --loc 500 --files 15 --quiet | wc -l` prints `1`.\n- **SC-002**: `bash recommend-level.sh --loc 500 --files 15 | wc -l` prints `16`, and that output matches the pre-change capture byte for byte.\n- **SC-003**: For `--loc 50 --files 2`, `--loc 500 --files 15` and `--loc 500 --files 15 --auth --api --db --architectural`, the quiet line equals the level line the default report prints.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | `bash` 3.2 or newer | The script cannot run at all on an older shell; nothing new is introduced | The script already targets that floor and uses `set -euo pipefail` today |\n| Risk | The default report regresses while the shared level-line helper is extracted | Med | Capture three baseline invocations before editing and diff them after |\n| Risk | A caller assumes `--quiet` also silences stderr | Low | Document stdout-only scope and test an error path with `--quiet` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: `--quiet` adds no measurable time; the quiet path is one `printf` and skips the report, so it can only be faster than the default run.\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: N/A - the flag reads no input, opens no file and handles no secret; it adds no attack surface.\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: Output is deterministic for identical arguments; the change adds no state, no file I/O and no randomness.\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: `--quiet` with no `--loc` and no `--files` exits 1 with the existing usage error on stderr and writes nothing to stdout.\n- Maximum length: `--loc 100000 --files 5000` saturates every factor at its weight cap and still prints one line, with the same shape as any other quiet run.\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- External service failure: N/A - the script calls no external service.\n- Network timeout: N/A - the script performs no network access.\n\n### State Transitions\n- Partial completion: N/A - the script is a single pass with no persisted state.\n- Session expiry: N/A - the script has no session.\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 4/25 | Files: 1, LOC: ~25, Systems: 1 |\n| Risk | 5/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N (the default report is preserved) |\n| Research | 4/20 | Read one script and capture three baselines |\n| Multi-Agent | 0/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 2/15 | Dependencies: 0 |\n| **Total** | **15/100** | **Level 3 (assigned by dispatch)** |\n\nThe measured score is 15/100, well under the 70-point Level 3 threshold. The packet is authored at\nLevel 3 because the dispatch fixed the level before authoring began, and the score is recorded as\nmeasured rather than inflated to match the contract. The documentation contract, not the change's\nsize, is what the level describes here.\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | Extracting the shared level-line helper changes the default report | H | L | Add the helper first, diff the three baselines immediately, and keep the helper's format string identical to the current `echo` |\n| R-002 | A caller expects `--quiet` to hide stderr warnings | M | M | State stdout-only scope in `--help` and in this spec; test an error path under `--quiet` |\n| R-003 | `--quiet --json` silently picks a winner the caller did not expect | M | L | Define `--json` precedence explicitly, document it, and test the pair |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: One-line answer for scripts (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** scaffolder or shell test that needs only the level, **I want** `--quiet` to print the recommended-level line alone, **so that** I can read the answer with `$(...)` instead of parsing a report.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: Unchanged report for humans (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** developer running the script by hand, **I want** the default report to stay exactly as it is, **so that** a convenience flag cannot cost me the breakdown I read today.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-003: Predictable composition with the other flags (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** caller that combines output flags, **I want** `--json` and `--help` to keep their current behavior when `--quiet` is present, **so that** adding the flag never changes an existing pipeline's output.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- None blocking. Whether `--quiet` deserves a `-q` alias is deliberately out of scope here; a caller that needs one can ask for a follow-up packet.\n- None blocking. Whether stderr should ever be silenced is a separate decision; this packet leaves stderr untouched.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `decision-record.md`\n\n---\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":1,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":1}}],"locations":[{"path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8f3d32b7e3/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md"}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w8f3d32b7e3/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"edit"}}
